## Strengths
- Accurate code references: `Makefile` include order, `Makefile.docker`'s parse-time `VERSION` default, README headings, and the `## Unreleased` section all verified against the actual files.
- The README content matches the implementation: the `sun` / `nukedev` / `nukeprod` aliases are correct against `agent-task-executor/hack/cluster-alias.sh`, and the runner behavior (Job render, apply, wait, log lines, Docker Hub push, bootstrap script) matches the copied sources.
- Container-autonomy is handled correctly: the cluster and `buca` commands appear only as README content the agent writes, not as steps the agent executes; verification is limited to filesystem checks plus `make precommit`.
- Verification distinguishes before vs after: all four baseline greps return 0/fail today, confirming the checks are meaningful.
- Constraints are copied from the spec and include the "Do NOT commit" guard; the prompt is correctly scoped to documentation only and explicitly forbids touching the build files, `hack/`, `.dark-factory.yaml`, and `bborbe/nuke`.
- Relevant plugin docs (`changelog-guide.md`, `documentation-guide.md`) and the project DoD are referenced rather than restated.

## Summary
This is a well-scoped, accurate documentation prompt with correct code references and container-safe verification. The main gap is verification coverage: requirement 4 (the two parse-time traps) and requirement 1's section placement are not checked, so an agent could omit them and still pass. Add those two checks; the remaining items (absolute plugin-doc paths, line-number hints, objective WHY) are quality improvements, not blockers.

